eft.** That is a real problem for a closed-source product.\n\n- **Distribution triggers the obligations.** If you ship a binary, installer, container image, or on-device software that includes or links `somelib`, GPL-3.0 generally requires the combined work to be released under GPL-3.0. You would have to provide the corresponding source and license it under the same terms. That conflicts with keeping the product closed.\n- **GPL-3.0 adds restrictions beyond GPL-2.0.** It includes an anti-tivoization clause (for consumer devices, you must provide installation information) and a patent license grant.\n- **Hosting it yourself may be different.** If you only run it as a hosted service and never distribute it, plain GPL-3.0 doesn't require releasing source. That loophole is closed by AGPL, and `somelib` is not AGPL. Check this against how you actually deliver the product.\n\n**What you can do about it** (in rough order of preference)\n1. Replace `somelib` with a permissively licensed alternative.\n2. Ask the `somelib` authors for a commercial or alternative license.\n3. Isolate it as a separate process that talks to your code over a CLI or network boundary. This is legally gray, so get counsel to review it before relying on it.\n4. Drop the feature that needs it.\n\n**Not a concern**\n- `requests` (Apache-2.0): permissive. You need to include the license text and any NOTICE file, and keep the attribution.\n- `left-pad` (MIT): permissive. You only need to include the copyright and license notice.\n\n**Caveats**\n- `deps.txt` lists only direct dependencies. Transitive dependencies can carry their own licenses, including copyleft ones, so scan the full resolved tree (for example with `pip-licenses` or the equivalent for your ecosystem).\n- The license strings in the file are declared labels. I haven't verified them against the packages' actual metadata or source.\n- This is a technical read, not legal advice. Have counsel confirm before you ship, especially if you choose the isolation route.\n\nIf you give me the lockfile or a way to resolve the full tree, I can check the transitive dependencies too.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}